Article 13
(1) Regional Government implements efforts to protect children and women victims of violence through:
a. implementing protection policies for children and women victims of violence applied by the government;
b. establishing policies, programs, and protection activities for children and women victims of violence;
c. establishment of PPT;
d. guaranteeing the implementation of service facilitation to victims;
e. pursuing effectiveness and efficiency for the victim recovery process; and
f. pursuing the creation of cooperation and coordination in victim recovery efforts.
(2) In order to implement the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, the regional government establishes action programs and activities for the protection of children and women in 1 (one) Regional Action Plan as the basis for regional apparatus in implementing protection for women and children victims of violence.
(3) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), constitutes part of the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
(4) The Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 8
Neglect as referred to in Article 4 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of a child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obligated to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by means of restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Article 7
Sexual violence as referred to in Article 4 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or
d. forced sexual intercourse with another person for commercial purposes and or specific purposes.

Article 14
(1) The obligations and responsibilities of the community as referred to in Article 11 letter b, shall be implemented in the form of community participation.
(2) The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. establishing family partners at the kelurahan level;
b. establishing child and women protection units within community organizations;
c. conducting socialization of children's rights and women's rights independently;
d. providing first aid to victims; and e. reporting to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
(3) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, social community institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, private sector, and mass media.
(4) The form of community participation as referred to in paragraph (2), shall be implemented responsibly in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

CHAPTER II
PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES
Article 2
The implementation of protection for women and children victims of violence, is carried out based on the principles of:
a. respect for human rights;
b. justice;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. protection of victims;

Article 8
Neglect as referred to in Article 4 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of a child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after a child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts that neglect a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obligated to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by means of restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Article 3
(1) The objectives of providing protection for children and women victims of violence are to:
a. prevent violence against children and women;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against children and women;
c. protect and provide a sense of security for children and women;
d. provide services to children and women victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
(2) The objectives of providing protection for children and women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:
a. prevention;
h. services and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

Article 23
(1) Forms of services provided to children and women victims of violence, are as follows:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by regional apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. social affairs;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and 14
d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In carrying out duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other regency/city governments, the community, families and parents.
(4) Further provisions regarding procedures for services, and handling of children and women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.
